//? Title: ALOPEX: Gradient-Free Optimization by Correlation
//? Subtitle: The drift law, the lattice it lives on, and a median regression of fuel economy on weight with datamunge's algorithms::alopex_minimize
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
ALOPEX (Harth & Tzanakou, 1974) minimises a cost function using only
evaluations of the cost. Every parameter moves by \(\pm\delta\) on every step.
The direction is a biased coin flip, and the bias comes from the correlation
between the parameter's last move and the change in cost that followed. We
derive the expected step. On average ALOPEX does gradient descent: when
correlations are small next to the temperature, the learning rate is
\(\delta^3/2T\); when they are large, it moves by the sign of the gradient. A
second, simpler argument shows that the iterates live on a lattice of spacing
\(\delta\), so the step size, not the run length, fixes the attainable
precision. Both results are checked on a problem without a gradient: a
least-absolute-deviation (median) regression of `mpg` on `wt` for the 32 cars
in `dm::datasets::mtcars()`. With \(\delta=0.02\) and 20 000 evaluations,
ALOPEX reaches the best point on its lattice, 0.007% above the exact optimum
found by `stats::QuantileRegression`. The measured ratio of correlation to
\(\delta^2\times\)gradient is 0.98 and 0.95 for the two coefficients, against
a predicted 1.
)

\toc

> The data: fuel economy against weight

The 1974 *Motor Trend* data has fuel consumption (`mpg`, miles per US
gallon) and weight (`wt`, in 1000 lb) for 32 cars. Heavier cars use more
fuel, but a handful of light economy cars (the Fiat 128, Honda Civic and
Toyota Corolla) get far more miles per gallon than a straight line through
the rest predicts. A *median* regression, which minimises absolute rather
than squared residuals, is less swayed by such points.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  std::print("{} cars.\n\n{}\n", cars.nrows(),
             cars | dm::select({"model", "wt", "mpg"})
                  | dm::arrange(dm::desc("mpg"))
                  | dm::slice_head(5)
                  | dm::to_md(3));
  std::print("\n{}\n",
             cars | dm::summarise(agg::mean("wt", "mean wt"), agg::median("wt", "median wt"),
                                  agg::mean("mpg", "mean mpg"), agg::median("mpg", "median mpg"),
                                  agg::sd("mpg", "sd mpg"))
                  | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
