> The method

>> The correlation rule

Let \(E(w)\) be a cost over parameters \(w\in\mathbb{R}^d\). ALOPEX keeps the
previous move \(\Delta w(n-1)\) and the cost change it caused,
\(\Delta E(n-1)=E(w(n-1))-E(w(n-2))\). For each parameter it forms the
correlation

$$
C_i(n)=\Delta w_i(n-1)\,\Delta E(n-1),
$$
\alttext(C sub i of n equals delta w sub i of n minus 1, times delta E of n minus 1.)

and then moves every parameter at once:

$$
\Delta w_i(n)=\begin{cases}+\delta & \text{with probability } p_i=\dfrac{1}{1+e^{C_i(n)/T}},\\[4pt] -\delta & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(Delta w sub i of n is plus delta with probability p sub i equal to 1 over 1 plus e to the C sub i over T, and minus delta otherwise.)

If raising \(w_i\) last time raised the cost, then \(C_i>0\), \(p_i<\tfrac12\),
and a decrease becomes more likely. If it lowered the cost, \(C_i<0\) and the
move tends to be repeated. The temperature \(T\) sets how much the
correlation matters. With `temperature <= 0` datamunge sets \(T\) at each
step to the mean of \(|C_i|\), which keeps \(C_i/T\) of order one whatever
the scale of the cost. In that mode `cooling` is not used.

```
ALOPEX(E, w, delta, T):
    dw = 0;  dE = 0;  E_old = E(w)
    repeat n times:
        for each i:
            C_i  = dw_i * dE
            dw_i = (uniform() < 1 / (1 + exp(C_i / T))) ? +delta : -delta
        w = w + dw
        E_new = E(w);  dE = E_new - E_old;  E_old = E_new
        keep the best w seen;  cool T
```

One step costs one evaluation of \(E\), whatever \(d\) is. Finite-difference
gradient descent needs \(d+1\).

>> The drift law: ALOPEX is gradient descent on average

Condition on the current correlation. The expected move is

$$
\mathbb{E}[\Delta w_i\mid C_i]=\delta\,(2p_i-1)=\delta\,\frac{1-e^{C_i/T}}{1+e^{C_i/T}}=-\delta\tanh\!\Big(\frac{C_i}{2T}\Big).
$$
\alttext(The expected move given C sub i is delta times 2 p sub i minus 1, which equals minus delta times tanh of C sub i over 2 T.)

Now relate \(C_i\) to the gradient \(g=\nabla E\). For a small step,
\(\Delta E\approx\sum_j g_j\,\Delta w_j\), so

$$
C_i=\Delta w_i\,\Delta E\approx g_i\,\Delta w_i^2+\sum_{j\ne i}g_j\,\Delta w_i\,\Delta w_j
=\delta^2 g_i+\sum_{j\ne i}g_j\,\Delta w_i\,\Delta w_j .
$$
\alttext(C sub i is approximately g sub i times delta w sub i squared plus the sum over j not equal to i of g sub j delta w sub i delta w sub j, which equals delta squared g sub i plus the same cross terms.)

The first term is exact because \(\Delta w_i^2=\delta^2\) always. Each cross
term is a product of two \(\pm\delta\) signs. Nearly independent fair coins
make it average to zero, so

$$
\mathbb{E}[C_i]\approx\delta^2\,g_i .
$$
\alttext(The expected value of C sub i is approximately delta squared times g sub i.)

This is a central-difference estimate of the gradient, computed one
coordinate at a time but paid for with one evaluation for all coordinates.
The cross terms are the price: they add noise with variance
\(\delta^4\sum_{j\ne i}g_j^2\), which grows with \(d\). Substituting into the
drift gives two regimes:

$$
\mathbb{E}[\Delta w_i]\approx
\begin{cases}
-\dfrac{\delta^3}{2T}\,g_i, & |C_i|\ll T \quad\text{(gradient descent, rate } \delta^3/2T),\\[8pt]
-\delta\,\operatorname{sign}(g_i), & |C_i|\gg T \quad\text{(sign-gradient descent).}
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(The expected move is minus delta cubed over 2 T times g sub i when C is small compared with T, which is gradient descent with rate delta cubed over 2 T, and minus delta times the sign of g sub i when C is large, which is sign-gradient descent.)

The derivation uses only \(\Delta E\). Where \(E\) has kinks it still holds
with \(g\) any subgradient, because the cost is never differentiated.

>> The lattice: why the step size sets the precision

Every coordinate moves by exactly \(\pm\delta\) on every step. After \(n\)
steps from \(w(0)\),

$$
w_i(n)=w_i(0)+\delta\,k_i,\qquad k_i\in\mathbb{Z},\quad k_i\equiv n \pmod 2 .
$$
\alttext(w sub i of n equals w sub i of 0 plus delta times k sub i, where k sub i is an integer with the same parity as n.)

So the iterates never leave the lattice \(w(0)+\delta\mathbb{Z}^d\). Moreover,
all the \(k_i\) share one parity, so only half of the lattice is ever
reachable. However long the run, the best ALOPEX can return is

$$
E^\star_\delta=\min\{E(w(0)+\delta k): k\in\mathbb{Z}^d,\ k_1\equiv k_2\equiv\cdots\equiv k_d \pmod 2\}.
$$
\alttext(E star delta is the minimum of E over w of 0 plus delta k, for integer vectors k whose entries all have the same parity.)

For a cost that is Lipschitz near its minimiser, \(E^\star_\delta-E^\star=O(\delta)\).
A smaller \(\delta\) buys precision, but the drift law says each step covers
at most \(\delta\), so the walk takes at least \(\|w^\star-w(0)\|_\infty/\delta\)
steps to arrive. Precision and speed trade off through the single number
\(\delta\).

> Median regression without a gradient

The least-absolute-deviation fit of \(\text{mpg}=\beta_0+\beta_1\,\text{wt}\)
minimises

$$
E(\beta)=\sum_{i=1}^{32}\big|\,\text{mpg}_i-\beta_0-\beta_1\,\text{wt}_i\big| .
$$
\alttext(E of beta is the sum over the 32 cars of the absolute value of mpg i minus beta 0 minus beta 1 times wt i.)

\(E\) is convex and piecewise linear. Its gradient jumps whenever the line
passes through a data point, and its minimum is at a corner. This is the
\(\tau=\tfrac12\) case of quantile regression, which `stats::QuantileRegression`
solves exactly with a simplex. That gives a reference point. ALOPEX starts at
\(\beta=(0,0)\) with \(\delta=0.02\), automatic temperature and 20 000 steps.
Ordinary least squares (`stats::LM`) is shown for contrast.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alopex_fit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::dstruct::DataFrame cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto wt  = cars.get_column<double>("wt").values();
  const auto mpg = cars.get_column<double>("mpg").values();
  auto lad = [&](const std::vector<double>& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < wt.size(); ++i) s += std::fabs(mpg[i] - b[0] - b[1] * wt[i]);
    return s;
  };

  dm::algorithms::AlopexParameters p;
  p.step = 0.02; p.temperature = 0.0; p.iterations = 20000; p.seed = 1;
  const auto run = dm::algorithms::alopex_minimize(lad, 2, {0.0, 0.0}, p);
  const dm::stats::QuantileRegression qr(dm::formula("mpg ~ wt"), cars);
  const dm::stats::LM ols(dm::formula("mpg ~ wt"), cars);
  const auto& bq = qr.coefficients();
  const auto& bo = ols.coefficients();
  const auto& ba = run.best_parameters;

  std::print("| method | intercept | slope | sum abs residual | cost evaluations |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| ALOPEX (delta = 0.02) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {} |\n", ba[0], ba[1],
             run.best_cost, p.iterations + 1);
  std::print("| QuantileRegression, tau = 0.5 | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | n/a |\n", bq[0], bq[1], lad(bq));
  std::print("| LM (least squares) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | n/a |\n", bo[0], bo[1], lad(bo));
  std::print("\nALOPEX ends {:.4f} ({:.4f}%) above the exact LAD optimum, starting from cost {:.1f}.\n",
             run.best_cost - lad(bq), 100 * (run.best_cost - lad(bq)) / lad(bq), run.initial_cost);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(wt, mpg, "cars", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 4.0)
      .abline(ba[0], ba[1], dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0)
      .abline(bq[0], bq[1], dm::RGB{245, 158, 11}, 1.5)
      .abline(bo[0], bo[1], dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .legend({"ALOPEX LAD", "exact LAD", "least squares"},
              {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{245, 158, 11}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}})
      .title("Fuel economy against weight, mtcars")
      .x_label("weight (1000 lb)")
      .y_label("miles per gallon")
      .size(860, 520)
      .save_svg("build/alopex_fit.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | intercept | slope | sum abs residual | cost evaluations |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| ALOPEX (delta = 0.02) | 34.320 | -4.560 | 74.2947 | 20001 |
| QuantileRegression, tau = 0.5 | 34.232 | -4.539 | 74.2893 | n/a |
| LM (least squares) | 37.285 | -5.344 | 74.9005 | n/a |

ALOPEX ends 0.0054 (0.0072%) above the exact LAD optimum, starting from cost 642.9.
\image(figures/alopex_fit.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The 32 cars with three fitted lines. The thick blue ALOPEX line and the thin amber exact median-regression line coincide. The red least-squares line is steeper.)
\alttext(Scatter plot of miles per gallon against weight for 32 cars, falling from about 33 mpg near 1.5 thousand pounds to about 10 mpg near 5.4 thousand pounds. The ALOPEX and exact least-absolute-deviation lines lie on top of each other; the least-squares line is steeper and sits above them at low weight.)

The two LAD lines agree to within one lattice step in each coefficient. They
are flatter than least squares, which lets the light, high-mpg cars pull its
line up and so raises its slope magnitude by about 0.8 mpg per 1000 lb. Each
fit is optimal for its own loss: least squares has the larger sum of absolute
residuals here.

> Checking the theory

>> The lattice limits precision

For several step sizes the next block runs ALOPEX for 20 000 steps from
three seeds. It then searches the reachable half-lattice by brute force for
\(E^\star_\delta\), using the square \(30\le\beta_0\le38\),
\(-6\le\beta_1\le-3\), which contains the optimum. It also records the first
step at which the cost came within 1% of the exact optimum.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alopex_convergence.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::dstruct::DataFrame cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto wt  = cars.get_column<double>("wt").values();
  const auto mpg = cars.get_column<double>("mpg").values();
  auto lad = [&](const std::vector<double>& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < wt.size(); ++i) s += std::fabs(mpg[i] - b[0] - b[1] * wt[i]);
    return s;
  };
  const double best = lad(dm::stats::QuantileRegression(dm::formula("mpg ~ wt"), cars).coefficients());

  std::print("| delta | lattice optimum E*_delta | ALOPEX best (3 seeds) | steps to within 1% (3 seeds) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<std::pair<double, dm::RGB>> shown = {
      {0.005, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}}, {0.02, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}}, {0.1, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}}};
  for (double delta : {0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1}) {
    double lattice = 1e300;  // brute force over k with k0 = k1 (mod 2)
    for (long a = std::lround(30 / delta); a <= std::lround(38 / delta); ++a)
      for (long b = std::lround(-6 / delta); b <= std::lround(-3 / delta); ++b)
        if ((a - b) % 2 == 0) lattice = std::min(lattice, lad({a * delta, b * delta}));
    std::string bests, hits;
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2, 3}) {
      dm::algorithms::AlopexParameters p;
      p.step = delta; p.temperature = 0.0; p.iterations = 20000; p.seed = seed;
      const auto run = dm::algorithms::alopex_minimize(lad, 2, {0.0, 0.0}, p);
      const auto& h = run.cost_history;
      const auto it = std::ranges::find_if(h, [&](double c) { return c < 1.01 * best; });
      bests += std::format("{}{:.4f}", seed > 1 ? ", " : "", run.best_cost);
      hits += std::format("{}{}", seed > 1 ? ", " : "",
                          it == h.end() ? std::string("never") : std::to_string(it - h.begin() + 1));
      if (seed == 1)
        for (const auto& [d, colour] : shown)
          if (d == delta) {
            std::vector<double> x, y;
            for (std::size_t n = 0; n < h.size(); n += (n < 1000 ? 1 : 10)) {
              x.push_back(std::log10(n + 1.0));
              y.push_back(std::log10(h[n] - best));
            }
            plot.lines(x, y, std::format("delta = {}", delta), colour, 1.2);
          }
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {} | {} |\n", delta, lattice, bests, hits);
  }
  std::print("\nExact optimum (QuantileRegression): {:.4f}.\n", best);
  plot.title("Excess cost along the run, seed 1")
      .x_label("log10 step")
      .y_label("log10 (E - E*)")
      .size(860, 500)
      .save_svg("build/alopex_convergence.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| delta | lattice optimum E*_delta | ALOPEX best (3 seeds) | steps to within 1% (3 seeds) |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |
| 0.005 | 74.2908 | 86.2653, 78.6371, 77.7486 | never, never, never |
| 0.01 | 74.2947 | 74.5101, 74.2947, 74.2947 | 12538, 14612, 11824 |
| 0.02 | 74.2947 | 74.2947, 74.2947, 74.2947 | 6743, 8721, 6717 |
| 0.05 | 74.3052 | 74.3052, 74.3052, 74.3052 | 3691, 3577, 4589 |
| 0.1 | 74.3656 | 74.3656, 74.3656, 74.3656 | 3046, 2780, 2214 |

Exact optimum (QuantileRegression): 74.2893.
\image(figures/alopex_convergence.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The excess cost \(E(w(n))-E^\star\) of the current iterate on log-log axes. Large steps arrive quickly but rattle well above the optimum. Small steps descend slowly but get closer.)
\alttext(Three curves of log10 excess cost against log10 step, all starting near 10 to the 2.75. The red curve for delta 0.1 falls first and from about 10 to the 3.5 steps fluctuates between excess costs of about 0.1 and 20. The blue curve for delta 0.02 drops sharply near 10 to the 3.8 steps and then fluctuates between about 0.01 and 1. The black curve for delta 0.005 falls slowest and is still descending at 20 000 steps.)

For \(\delta\ge0.02\) all three seeds return exactly the lattice optimum,
and at \(\delta=0.01\) two of the three do. Different random walks find the
same corner of the same lattice, so the remaining error is set by
\(\delta\), as derived. The excess
\(E^\star_\delta-E^\star\) falls from 0.076 at \(\delta=0.1\) to 0.016 at
\(0.05\) and 0.005 at \(0.02\) and \(0.01\). (At \(0.01\) the coarser
optimum happens to lie on the finer lattice too.) The cost of precision is
travel time. With \(\delta=0.005\) the walk needs at least \(34.2/0.005\approx6800\)
steps just to reach the optimal intercept. None of its seeds came within
1% of the optimum in 20 000 steps; their best costs were 78–86. Coming
within 1% took about 2 200–3 000 steps at \(\delta=0.1\), 6 700–8 700 at
\(0.02\) and 11 800–14 600 at \(0.01\).

The figure also shows that the *current* iterate never settles. The
temperature tracks \(|C|\), so near the minimum the coin flips stay
informative but never become certain, and the walk wanders over a band of
lattice points around the optimum. That is why `alopex_minimize` returns the
best point seen, not the last one.

>> The path through parameter space

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alopex_paths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::dstruct::DataFrame cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto wt  = cars.get_column<double>("wt").values();
  const auto mpg = cars.get_column<double>("mpg").values();
  auto lad = [&](const std::vector<double>& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < wt.size(); ++i) s += std::fabs(mpg[i] - b[0] - b[1] * wt[i]);
    return s;
  };
  const auto bq = dm::stats::QuantileRegression(dm::formula("mpg ~ wt"), cars).coefficients();
  const auto bo = dm::stats::LM(dm::formula("mpg ~ wt"), cars).coefficients();

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::print("| delta | turning step | intercept there | slope there | final b0 + 3.325 b1 |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto [delta, colour] : std::vector<std::pair<double, dm::RGB>>{
           {0.02, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}}, {0.1, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}}}) {
    dm::algorithms::AlopexParameters p;
    p.step = delta; p.temperature = 0.0; p.iterations = 20000; p.seed = 1; p.record_path = true;
    const auto run = dm::algorithms::alopex_minimize(lad, 2, {0.0, 0.0}, p);
    std::vector<double> b0{0.0}, b1{0.0};
    std::size_t turn = 0;  // step where the slope peaks
    for (std::size_t n = 0; n < run.parameter_history.size(); n += 5) {
      b0.push_back(run.parameter_history[n][0]);
      b1.push_back(run.parameter_history[n][1]);
    }
    plot.lines(b0, b1, std::format("delta = {}", delta), colour, 1.0);
    for (std::size_t n = 0; n < run.parameter_history.size(); ++n)
      if (run.parameter_history[n][1] > run.parameter_history[turn][1]) turn = n;
    const auto& w = run.parameter_history[turn];
    const auto& b = run.best_parameters;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} |\n", delta, turn + 1, w[0], w[1],
               b[0] + 3.325 * b[1]);
  }
  plot.points({bq[0]}, {bq[1]}, "exact LAD", dm::RGB{245, 158, 11}, 7.0)
      .points({bo[0]}, {bo[1]}, "least squares", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7.0)
      .title("ALOPEX iterates from (0, 0)")
      .x_label("intercept")
      .y_label("slope")
      .size(860, 520)
      .save_svg("build/alopex_paths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| delta | turning step | intercept there | slope there | final b0 + 3.325 b1 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.02 | 530 | 1.20 | 4.84 | 19.16 |
| 0.1 | 82 | 0.20 | 5.20 | 19.24 |
\image(figures/alopex_paths.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Every fifth iterate of the seed-1 runs, in the \((\beta_0,\beta_1)\) plane. Both walks first raise the slope with the intercept almost fixed, then turn and slide down a narrow valley to the LAD optimum, the amber point. The \(\delta=0.1\) walk keeps wandering along the valley, past the least-squares point.)
\alttext(Line plot of slope against intercept. Both paths start at the origin. They first climb almost vertically, the slope rising to about 5 while the intercept stays between 0 and 2. Then they turn and travel down and to the right along a narrow straight valley, the slope falling to about minus 4.5 as the intercept grows to about 34, where the amber exact LAD point sits. The red path for delta 0.1 is a thick scribble from intercept 30 to 40 along the valley, passing the black least-squares point at intercept 37.3 and slope minus 5.3; the blue path for delta 0.02 is thinner and stays close to the amber point.)

The first leg shows the cross-term noise from the drift law. At
\(\beta=(0,0)\) every residual is positive, so
\(g=-(32,\ \sum_k\text{wt}_k)=-(32,\ 102.95)\), and both coefficients should
rise. But a joint move changes \(E\) by about \(-32\Delta\beta_0-103\Delta\beta_1\),
so the sign of \(\Delta E\), and with it the sign of *both* correlations, is
set almost entirely by \(\Delta\beta_1\). \(C_1\) is informative and the
slope climbs steadily. \(C_0\) is mostly the cross term
\(g_1\Delta\beta_0\Delta\beta_1\), which is as likely to be positive as
negative, so the intercept barely moves. In the \(\delta=0.02\) run the
slope climbs to 4.84 by step 530, while the intercept has reached only 1.20.

By then the line \(\text{mpg}=1.2+4.84\,\text{wt}\) cuts through the middle
of the data, with the light cars *above* the line and the heavy ones
below, and the slope's subgradient changes sign. From there the walk
follows the valley floor. Along it the fitted line keeps passing near the
centre of the cloud: at the end, \(\beta_0+3.325\,\beta_1\), the fit at the
median weight, is 19.16 and 19.24 for the two runs, against a median `mpg`
of 19.2. The valley is long and narrow because the weights do not straddle
zero (they run from 1.5 to 5.4). Many lines through the centre of the
cloud fit almost equally well, and only the tilt has to be found.

>> The drift law in the data

The decisive prediction is \(\mathbb{E}[C_i]\approx\delta^2 g_i\). The
`alopex_correlations` helper rebuilds every \(C_i(n)\) from a recorded run.
The LAD subgradient \(g\) at \(w(n-2)\), the point where the move behind
\(C_i(n)\) was made, has a closed form:
\(g_0=-\sum_k\operatorname{sign}(r_k)\) and
\(g_1=-\sum_k\operatorname{sign}(r_k)\,\text{wt}_k\). We compare the two
over the whole run and over its first 2 000 steps, the descent phase.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const dm::dstruct::DataFrame cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const auto wt  = cars.get_column<double>("wt").values();
  const auto mpg = cars.get_column<double>("mpg").values();
  auto lad = [&](const std::vector<double>& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < wt.size(); ++i) s += std::fabs(mpg[i] - b[0] - b[1] * wt[i]);
    return s;
  };
  auto subgradient = [&](const std::vector<double>& b) {
    std::vector<double> g{0.0, 0.0};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < wt.size(); ++i) {
      const double r = mpg[i] - b[0] - b[1] * wt[i];
      const double s = (r > 0) - (r < 0);
      g[0] -= s;
      g[1] -= s * wt[i];
    }
    return g;
  };

  const double delta = 0.02;
  const std::vector<double> init{0.0, 0.0};
  dm::algorithms::AlopexParameters p;
  p.step = delta; p.temperature = 0.0; p.iterations = 20000; p.seed = 1; p.record_path = true;
  const auto run = dm::algorithms::alopex_minimize(lad, 2, init, p);
  const auto C = dm::algorithms::alopex_correlations(run, init);

  std::print("| steps | coefficient | mean C | mean delta^2 g | ratio | sign agreement |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (auto [lo, hi] : std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>>{{2, 2000}, {2, 20000}})
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
      double sc = 0, sg = 0;
      std::size_t agree = 0, nonzero = 0;
      for (std::size_t n = lo; n < hi; ++n) {
        const double g = delta * delta * subgradient(run.parameter_history[n - 2])[i];
        sc += C[n][i];
        sg += g;
        if (C[n][i] != 0 && g != 0) { ++nonzero; agree += (C[n][i] > 0) == (g > 0); }
      }
      std::print("| {}-{} | {} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} | {:.3f} | {:.1f}% |\n", lo, hi,
                 i == 0 ? "intercept" : "slope", sc / (hi - lo), sg / (hi - lo), sc / sg,
                 100.0 * agree / nonzero);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| steps | coefficient | mean C | mean delta^2 g | ratio | sign agreement |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2-2000 | intercept | -5.308e-03 | -4.497e-03 | 1.180 | 80.3% |
| 2-2000 | slope | -7.585e-03 | -7.336e-03 | 1.034 | 71.1% |
| 2-20000 | intercept | -9.709e-04 | -9.952e-04 | 0.976 | 59.6% |
| 2-20000 | slope | -6.153e-04 | -6.456e-04 | 0.953 | 80.5% |
// result_end

Over the full run the ratio of mean correlation to mean \(\delta^2 g\) is
close to the predicted 1 for both coefficients. In the descent phase it is
somewhat above 1 for the intercept. There, consecutive moves are not
independent coins: the walk is pushing hard in one direction, so the cross
terms \(\Delta w_0\Delta w_1\) have a non-zero mean. The sign-agreement
column shows how noisy any single correlation is. A single \(C_i\) has the
same sign as the subgradient only 60–80% of the time, and for the
intercept over the full run the figure is 60%. The drift emerges from
averaging many steps, which is one reason ALOPEX needs thousands of
evaluations for a two-parameter problem.

> Summary

- ALOPEX moves every parameter by \(\pm\delta\) on every step, choosing the
  sign by a logistic coin whose bias is the correlation
  \(C_i=\Delta w_i\Delta E\). The expected move is \(-\delta\tanh(C_i/2T)\).
- Since \(\mathbb{E}[C_i]\approx\delta^2\partial E/\partial w_i\), the method
  is stochastic gradient descent, with rate \(\delta^3/2T\) for small
  correlations and sign-gradient steps for large ones. It never
  differentiates \(E\), so kinks do not matter. One evaluation drives all
  \(d\) coordinates, at the cost of cross-term noise that grows with \(d\).
- The iterates live on the half-lattice \(w(0)+\delta\mathbb{Z}^d\) with
  equal parities, so \(\delta\) fixes the precision and longer runs cannot
  beat \(E^\star_\delta\). On the mtcars median regression, 11 of the 12
  runs with \(\delta\ge0.01\) returned \(E^\star_\delta\) exactly.
- With \(\delta=0.02\), ALOPEX matched the exact `QuantileRegression` LAD fit
  to within 0.007% in 20 000 evaluations. That is accurate, but far slower
  than a method that exploits the problem's structure. ALOPEX is for black
  boxes, not for problems with a known convex form.

```{cpp, eval="no"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

dm::algorithms::AlopexParameters p;
p.step        = 0.02;   // lattice spacing: sets precision and speed
p.temperature = 0.0;    // auto: T = mean |C| at each step
p.iterations  = 20000;
p.record_path = true;   // keep iterates for alopex_correlations()
auto run = dm::algorithms::alopex_minimize(cost, dim, start, p);
// run.best_parameters, run.best_cost, run.cost_history, run.temperature_history
```
